(2) Is naive_clamp (GHRS ClippedReLU) a faithful [0,1] clamp in finite precision?
================================================================================
  paper example (fp16): x-1=2049 rounds to 2048, so naive_clamp(2050) = 2   (NOT in [0,1])

  Range check of both clamps over finite representable inputs.
  bfloat16/float16: every finite value; float32: all 2^32 bit patterns;
  float64: 2*10^7 random finite bit patterns plus 2^k, 2^k+-1 for k=0..1023.
  bfloat16 :       65280 finite inputs | ClippedReLU outside [0,1]:      64 (values [2.0]) | clamp01 outside [0,1]: 0
  float16  :       63488 finite inputs | ClippedReLU outside [0,1]:     512 (values [2.0]) | clamp01 outside [0,1]: 0
  float32  :  4278190080 finite inputs | ClippedReLU outside [0,1]: 4194304 (values [2.0]) | clamp01 outside [0,1]: 0
  float64  :    19994450 finite inputs | ClippedReLU outside [0,1]:    2455 (values [2.0]) | clamp01 outside [0,1]: 0
  => ClippedReLU is not a finite-precision clamp; clamp01 confines every tested input
     to [0,1], as Lemma 1 proves for every finite representable input.

